00:07:53.500 --> 00:08:00.460
Imagine you added a broad market ETF to your TFSA last month after seeing steady inflows into large cap names.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:11.740
Your purchase executed at the prevailing ask yet the real mechanism at work is that index funds must buy the exact constituents in market cap weights regardless of valuation.

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:16.900
Creating mechanical demand that can lift prices even when earnings growth slows.

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:28.980
Over the past decade this loop has concentrated gains in the largest holdings but professionals still set marginal prices through active trading that remains many times larger than net passive inflows.

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:35.580
The pro tip is to watch the ratio of ETF creation redemption activity to total trading volume.

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:42.580
When that ratio spikes above 15% on a single name it often signals the mechanical bid is dominating.

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:53.980
Most retail investors assume index buying is always supportive yet the concrete risk appears when active managers collectively step away and the self reinforcing looper versus.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:09:03.440
Track the percentage of S&amp;P 500 volume still executed by discretionary accounts rather than assuming passive dominance will continue indefinitely.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:17.480
Canadian investors can monitor this dynamic through the TSX's own ETF creation data published monthly by the exchange which reveals whether mechanical flows are overwhelming fundamental price discovery in domestic names.

00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:30.820
When the ratio of passive to active volume exceeds historical norms rebalancing toward equal weight or factor based ETFs can reduce exposure to the largest constituents that benefit most from the mechanical bid.

